From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0200
Subject: nouveau: on the PS3, stream storage gets a GART pool of its own, slab
 retention gets a switch, and the GART slabs get a stats line

nouveau_mm suballocates every GART buffer from shared slabs by size class, so the storage the CPU
rewrites all the time (u_upload's stream buffers, so nv30's fragment programs since 0069 and its
index and vertex uploads, staging buffers, persistent and coherent maps, client arrays uploaded at
every draw) shares slab objects with long-lived GART buffers such as index buffers. On the PS3
(is_ps3):

- A second GART suballocator, mm_GART_stream, on the same size classes (nouveau_mm_ps3_init, at
  screen init once mm_GART is made). nouveau_buffer_gart_mm chooses the pool once per allocation,
  from the buffer's usage and flags: stream buffers, staging buffers, persistent and coherent ones
  and user-memory buffers go there, and so do transfer staging and readback areas
  (nouveau_transfer_staging). Everything else stays in mm_GART: index buffers made in GART, nv30's
  moves of index buffers to GART (a stream one goes to the stream pool), buffers that fell back from
  VRAM. NV30_PS3_GART_POOL=0: one pool, as before.
- NV30_PS3_MM_KEEP_SLAB=N: each size class of the two GART pools keeps N empty slabs for its next
  allocations and frees the slabs that empty past them, the oldest first (0 frees every slab that
  empties). The default is -1, every one kept: nouveau_mm never freed a slab, one that emptied went
  on its bucket's free list and stayed there until the screen's end (nouveau_mm_destroy), and that
  stays so. Freeing isn't the default because each free is a GEM close, and a load that crosses a
  slab boundary every frame would make and free a slab every frame; a non-default N gives a peak's
  slabs (a level load) back, for measuring. With N set, a live buffer gets the memory it got before,
  except that its bucket has at most N empty slabs to take, and a slab's object closes only at its
  last reference, so after any pending submission that uses it. The screen's end frees the kept slabs.
- NV30_PS3_GART_STATS=1: a stderr line at the screen's end, for each pool: the bytes of slab objects
  it holds, their peak, the slabs made and freed and the empty ones kept. The counts change only
  when a slab is made or freed, so they cost nothing per allocation.

GART cost: the split adds at most one part-used slab for each size class both pools use at once (in
practice the small classes' 4-128 KiB slabs, and a 4 MiB one only when both pools hold 1 MiB
buffers). mm_VRAM and other GPUs keep every slab and one GART pool, as before.

Forwarded: no

